Watchman Whiteboard is a modified copy of gr-whiteboard by GoodRelax
(https://github.com/GoodRelax/gr-whiteboard, commit 6d6fd73), used under the Apache License 2.0
(LICENSE). gr-whiteboard's own notice is kept in NOTICE.

Imported 2026-11-17: skills/, test/, LICENSE, NOTICE and package.json. Not imported: the Claude Code
plugin files (.claude-plugin/, .mcp.json), docs/ and plan/.

Changes made by Incord AI, newest last:
- 2026-11-17: removed the two tests of the Claude Code plugin files (test/unit/mcp-libs.test.js),
  which are not shipped; Watchman's memory engine wires the board's MCP server into every agent.
- 2026-11-17: renamed to Watchman Whiteboard (the owner's choice): the page title, status line and
  toolbar label, the local certificate names, the screenshot temp folder, package.json, and the MCP
  server name (board-control -> whiteboard), with the two tests that pin them. The status line says
  "the agent's answers", since any agent may draw. The source link written into saved boards and the
  origin of the thinking frameworks still name GoodRelax.
- 2026-11-17: the page can load its libraries from page/vendor (staged by Watchman's build) instead of
  esm.sh, so the board works offline: board-api.js gains vendorFile and a GET /vendor/<file> route
  (known file types only, never outside the folder), and its content security policy then names no
  other site. Without a staged vendor folder it loads from esm.sh as before. Tests in
  test/unit/config-lan.test.js.
- 2026-11-17: in an agent turn Watchman started (WATCHMAN_APP_ENDPOINT and WATCHMAN_APP_TOKEN set),
  open_board opens the board in a Watchman browser tab (new skills/talk-on-board/scripts/lib/
  watchman-tab.js; mcp.js openBoard reports browser=watchman-tab); the browser of the OS only outside
  Watchman. Tests: test/unit/watchman-tab.test.js and a new case in test/integration/mcp.test.js.
- 2026-11-17: client.js exits only after standard output has taken its reply (writeAndExit): on macOS
  a pipe is written asynchronously, and process.exit right after the write cut a reply over 8 KB
  off (test/integration/client-log.test.js "sent prints it in full" failed on macOS only).
- 2026-11-17: the tests that make temp folders or start a Board Server run in a private temp folder
  removed when the file ends (test/support/private-temp.js), so the suite leaves nothing behind;
  before, 21 folders and the screenshot folder stayed in the system temp folder after every run.
- 2026-11-17: drawing animation and narration (the owner's choice). draw_mermaid, draw_table and
  draw_skeletons show their drawing like a person at a whiteboard: shapes traced, then arrows and lines
  growing, then text written letter by letter, a few seconds in all (new page/reveal.mjs, the pure half,
  served by board-api.js at GET /reveal.mjs; app.js revealDrawing). The frames are local Elements marked
  grwbAnimation that collectChanges never sends; the Job's reply waits for the animation, which stays
  below 40% of the Job timeout (board-hub.js now sends timeoutMs with draw and mermaid Jobs), and the
  final Elements are exactly as before. #anim=<speed> in the page link sets the speed (0 = none);
  prefers-reduced-motion and a hidden page turn it off. The three draw tools take an optional `say`
  (1 to 20 texts of 1 to 200 characters; mcp.js schemas, board-ops.js checkSay, server.js, the Job):
  the page draws one part per sentence and, as each part starts, emits the window event
  watchman-whiteboard:say {text, part, parts, jobId} and posts it to the parent or opener window.
  drawSkeletons now binds arrows to the Board shapes as they are after the animation. Tests:
  test/unit/reveal.test.js and new cases in board-ops, board-hub-ops, operations, server and mcp tests.
- 2026-11-17: the drawing animation follows the diagram's flow (the owner, after watching it): each
  shape is traced and its text written at once, then the arrow leaving it and its label, then its
  target, breadth first from the shapes no arrow comes into (reveal.mjs revealGroups); back arrows are
  drawn when their source is reached; free texts inside a shape or beside an arrow count as its label;
  tables row by row. `say` parts follow the same step groups. The first step was drawn during the
  animated scroll to the drawing: the animation now starts after the scroll, and frames follow
  reveal.mjs revealTimeline, which shows every step half drawn. window.__wbRevealLog records each step
  as it starts ({step, elementId, kind, at, jobId}) for checks in a browser.
- 2026-11-17: the board page shows only Watchman, none of Excalidraw's own branding (the owner's
  choice): app.js gives Excalidraw its own MainMenu (Export, Save as image, Search, Help, Clear canvas,
  theme, background; no "Excalidraw links"/Socials, collaboration, load/save-to-file or command
  palette) and its own WelcomeScreen ("Watchman Whiteboard" in place of the Excalidraw logo); the label
  "Mermaid to Excalidraw" reads "Mermaid to diagram"; the library's Publish item is hidden; index.html
  hides the library's "Browse libraries" button and the Help dialog's links to Excalidraw's sites (new
  page/brand.mjs, served at GET /brand.mjs). Excalidraw's MIT credit stays in NOTICE. Tests:
  test/unit/brand.test.js and a case in test/integration/server.test.js.
- 2026-11-17: outside an agent turn Watchman started (an agent in a plain terminal, a test over SSH),
  open_board shows the board in a tab of the running Watchman too (the owner's choice: the browser of
  the OS only when Watchman is not available). lib/watchman-tab.js finds Watchman as watchman-cli does,
  through runtime.json in its app-data folder (WATCHMAN_DATA_DIR first; else %APPDATA%,
  ~/Library/Application Support, or $XDG_DATA_HOME or ~/.local/share, then ai.incord.watchman), checks
  that its process runs and its endpoint is a loopback address, and asks the runtime method
  browser.openLocal with the owner token, only for a board on this computer. When Watchman is not
  running, does not answer or cannot show it, the browser of the OS opens the board and the reply says
  browser=opened reason="...". The order (the turn's endpoint, then runtime.json, then the system
  browser) is lib/watchman-tab.js showBoard, which mcp.js openBoard now calls. Tests: new cases in
  test/unit/watchman-tab.test.js and test/integration/mcp.test.js.
- 2026-11-17: the board follows Watchman's look (the owner: dark when Watchman is dark, with the same
  glass as its main app). showBoard gives Watchman the link with wm=1 in its hash (watchmanLink; the
  system browser gets the plain link); Watchman adds theme=dark|light and glass=1|0 and sends them again
  when its theme changes. New page/look.mjs (served at GET /look.mjs) reads them; app.js passes the
  theme to Excalidraw, makes the page and the canvas see-through with glass (only on the page: the
  Server never receives appState) and applies a new hash without a reload. Inside Watchman the menu has
  no theme or canvas-colour item (brand.mjs menuItems). The right-hand palette (Send, Auto-send, the
  agent's signal, the connection, Join) is no longer rendered (the owner: not usable): its Send reaches
  only an agent that watches the board with Claude Code's Monitor tool; the welcome text no longer
  names Send. Tests: test/unit/look.test.js, new cases in brand.test.js, watchman-tab.test.js and
  test/integration/server.test.js.
- 2026-11-17: a page counts only while it is there (Mac: Claude's open_board said pages=1 browser=skipped
  for a page that had gone, and its draw failed with NoPage). The page answers each ping with POST
  /api/pong (app.js); any post from a page marks it as there (board-hub.js touchPage). A page that
  answered no ping for 60 s is dropped at its next ping (board-api.js, STALE_MS), and the new
  POST /v1/pages/probe pings every page and drops the ones that do not answer within 1.5 s (hub.probe,
  server.js probePages; logged as PageStale). open_board counts only the pages that answer, and always
  asks Watchman to show the board: Watchman keeps one board tab and brings it forward; with no live page
  the link carries open=<ms> so a board tab still there reloads; a live page in another browser opens
  no system browser (watchman-tab.js showBoard systemBrowser). draw_mermaid, draw_table,
  draw_skeletons, edit_items, write_answer and take_screenshot with no live page show the board the same
  way, wait up to 8 s (GRWB_PAGE_WAIT_MS) for its page, then run once, else NoPage "no page is open,
  and none connected within 8 s" (mcp.js ensurePage). Tests: new cases in test/unit/board-hub.test.js,
  test/integration/server.test.js and test/integration/mcp.test.js.
- 2026-11-17: one board; a new drawing keeps the old one in the board's Library (the owner's choice).
  New scripts/lib/board-library.js. The Library is the board's, not one browser's: <data>/library.json
  (newest first, at most 100 items), sent in hello and in a `library` event, and a person's own library
  edits come back through POST /api/library (checkLibrary) and go to the other pages (app.js
  applyLibrary, onLibraryChange; board-hub.js setLibrary). The MCP Server names its agent session on
  every draw (drawer) and the three draw tools take an optional fresh. The rule (newDrawingDue): a
  drawing by another agent than the one whose drawing is on the board is new; the same agent's next
  drawing adds to its own; fresh=true starts anew, fresh=false adds; arrows bound to shapes on the board
  add; a request with no drawer (client.js) adds, as before. Before a new drawing the Server (a page must
  be there) puts the board's live elements into the Library as one item named "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM <first
  text from the top left>" (UTC), then deletes them all as one Operation "clear" that undo takes back
  (BoardCleared), then draws. Tests: test/unit/board-library.test.js, a new case in
  test/integration/server.test.js and in test/integration/mcp.test.js.
